# AttentionX Architecture

- This is a Chrome Manifest V3 extension built with TypeScript, React, and Vite.
- Treat X as an untrusted, frequently changing host page.
- Prefer semantic post attributes and Schema.org metadata over generated classes.
- A page-world observer may passively inspect cloned, allowlisted X JSON responses to extract public `rest_id`, username, and post IDs.
- Page-world may make authenticated X GraphQL calls (for example `SearchTimeline`) in the signed-in user's session to discover or verify NIP-39 proof posts without navigating the UI or scraping the DOM. Those calls may use session cookies (`ct0` CSRF), the public web bearer, and query ids learned from live traffic or client bundles. Forward only validated proof match results (`postId`, handle, proof text) — never raw GraphQL bodies, auth cookies, or bearer tokens — across the content boundary. Persist discoveries in IndexedDB `xIdentities`; do not invent kind `10011` events until the user publishes. Initiate GraphQL proof search only when `xIdentities` lacks a verified binding: (1) extension X-pane open / explicit self CHECK with `scanPage`, or (2) the user clicks Trust on another X account.
- Do not collect or persist `auth_token`, raw cookie strings, request headers, protected content, DMs, or unrelated personalized fields. The content script may derive only the signed-in account's numeric ID from the public `twid` cookie value (`u=<id>`).
- Never modify X's existing requests or responses; active GraphQL calls are separate extension-initiated fetches.
- Forward and persist only validated, normalized identity tuples from page-world observations.
- Proof-post submission requires a visible preview, explicit per-post confirmation, and active-account verification.
- Never perform any other X account action (no likes, follows, DMs, deletes, etc.).
- Keep the Nostr secret key out of content scripts and page-context code.
- Sign and query Nostr events in the background service worker with the unlocked vault-backed active account.
- Isolate injected UI with Shadow DOM and preserve X's existing behavior.
- Make DOM augmentation idempotent and compatible with SPA navigation.
- Publish trust, distrust, and cancellation as kind `32009` statements with stable `ext:twitter_id` and `ext:twitter_post` subjects; kind `1985` is retired and unsupported.
- Use `identity` as the default X-account context and `news:accuracy` as the default X-post context; keep question actions local-only.
- Merge and verify NIP-39 kind `10011` X links with matching `twitter` and `twitter_id` proof tags. Treat `10011` as the user's current claim (latest replaceable event) that narrows which X account is linked; accept it only after the referenced X proof post verifies. Persist durable Nostr↔X bindings in the IndexedDB `xIdentities` store — do not auto-create kind `10011` when a proof is discovered; the user publishes `10011` explicitly.
- In `xIdentities`, only a found X proof post may write `xProof*` fields, and only kind `10011` may write `nip39*` fields. A nip39 event must never invent or override proof fields. `state` becomes `verified` when both sides are present and column-aligned on the `xIdentities` row alone — status sync must not load kind `10011` events or run live oEmbed. Live VERIFY/publish paths remain separate.
- After every `xIdentities` field write, re-run status sync from the current row columns (independent of which side changed). Broadcast `X_IDENTITY_UPDATED` when derived status changes so the extension UI and x.com content script refresh immediately.
- Keep raw signed events, reducer indexes, relay cursors, identity records, and the durable outbox in IndexedDB; reserve `chrome.storage.local` for small settings and the encrypted key vault.
- Expose NIP-07 `window.nostr` only; do not implement WebLN / payments.
- Bound local WoT synchronization and traversal, preserve source evidence and truncation state, and never present it as an objective score.
- Treat proof generation and verification as backend capabilities; do not add composer posting until Phase D provides preview, explicit confirmation, and active-account verification.
- Document manifest permission or protocol schema changes.
- Run `npm run check` before handing off changes.
